1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,825
Two teams studied the same burnt bones from ancient Carthage, and reached conflicting conclusions.

2
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:08,410
They come from Carthage's tophet:

3
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:13,161
a sacred precinct where people buried urns of cremated infants, and young lambs.

4
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:18,389
Stones above many urns record vows to the gods Ba'al Hammon and Tanit.

5
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:22,759
Greek writers accused Carthage of sacrificing children.

6
00:00:22,759 --> 00:00:24,619
None of them was Carthaginian.

7
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:30,333
Patricia Smith's team concluded most of the infants died at one to one and a half months:

8
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:32,217
a pattern they read as sacrifice.

9
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:36,642
Jeffrey Schwartz's team concluded many died before or around birth:

10
00:00:36,642 --> 00:00:41,792
consistent, they argue, with natural deaths, though occasional sacrifice may have happened.

11
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:43,696
The dispute is method:

12
00:00:43,696 --> 00:00:50,039
how much fire shrinks bone, and whether it erases a tooth line that forms only after a baby's first week or two.

13
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:54,691
One twenty twenty-four expert review still called the evidence uncertain.

14
00:00:54,691 --> 00:00:57,625
Offered to the gods, or mourned and buried with care?

15
00:00:57,625 --> 00:00:58,733
We still don't know.

